  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Afghanistan Community engagement and accountability support &#x2013; Central Feedback Mechanism</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">510 providing technical support to IFRC will leverage the strengths of ARCS, and its partners to establish a robust feedback mechanism, enhancing community engagement and accountability, and leading to more responsive and effective humanitarian assistance in Afghanistan.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The primary beneficiaries are communities in Afghanistan affected by natural disasters and conflict, with a focus on marginalized groups such as women, children, and those in remote areas. ARCS staff and volunteers are also beneficiaries, as they will receive training and tools to manage and respond to feedback more effectively, which ultimately improves community support.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project is based in Afghanistan, targeting areas heavily affected by conflict and natural disasters, including earthquake and flood-affected regions like Herat. The centralized feedback mechanism is intended to be accessible across multiple regions, especially in areas with limited access to communication channels.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will implement a centralized digital feedback mechanism using tools like EspoCRM and Kobo Toolbox. Key steps include training ARCS staff, integrating feedback channels, conducting awareness campaigns, and building a sustainable support structure to ensure the feedback system continues after project completion. The Netherlands Red Cross (NLRC) will provide technical support, and IFRC will aid in training and monitoring.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To address significant issues in humanitarian aid delivery in Afghanistan. Due to prolonged conflict, frequent natural disasters, and socio-economic instability, Afghan communities, particularly the most vulnerable, have limited means to communicate their needs or provide feedback on aid effectiveness. This lack of engagement has led to inefficiencies, lower trust in aid organizations, and, ultimately, a reduced impact of humanitarian support. By implementing this feedback system, ARCS and its partners aim to create a more responsive, transparent, and accountable aid process, empowering communities to have a say in the support they receive and ensuring that resources are used in ways that directly meet local needs.</narrative>
  </description>
